readsb (3.16.15-4) stable; urgency=medium

  * sdr_soapy: fill the whole block. SoapySDRDevice_readStream() returns at
    most getStreamMTU() elements per call, but the reader called it once and
    treated the result as a complete block. That holds only for drivers whose
    MTU is >= Modes.sdr_buf_samples -- true for rtlsdr, false for others. On a
    LimeSDR Mini v2 the MTU is 2040 against a default buffer of 65536, so every
    block arrived about 3% full and messages straddling a boundary were lost.
    Measured on aryaos-c998 at 1090 MHz, same antenna and gain, before -> after:
    samples lost 63496 -> 0, "unusual size" warnings present -> none, Mode-S
    preambles 747933 -> 1377340.

  * sdr_soapy: a single SOAPY_SDR_OVERFLOW no longer ends reception. Any
    negative readStream() return previously broke out of the reader loop, so
    one driver-side overrun stopped the process receiving at all. An overflow
    means samples were dropped, not that the stream is dead.

  * NOT claimed: that a LimeSDR is now a good ADS-B receiver. On the test box
    it still decodes only a couple of messages. The Lime carries no 1090 MHz
    SAW filter or LNA and its own noise floor swamps the signal: measured 18
    samples >20dB over the median at 1090 MHz against 0 at a 1040 MHz control,
    i.e. real but very weak traffic, with SNR near 0 dB at every gain setting
    and the LNA already pinned at its 30 dB maximum. That is an RF sensitivity
    limit wanting an external filtered LNA, not a code change.

 -- Greg Albrecht <oss@snstac.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:30:00 +0000

readsb (3.16.15-3) stable; urgency=medium

  * sdr_soapy: --gain was applied ten times too large. readsb.c parses the
    option into Modes.gain as tenths of a dB, because librtlsdr's
    rtlsdr_set_tuner_gain() takes tenths. The SoapySDR path passed that value
    straight to SoapySDRDevice_setGain(), which takes plain dB, so every
    setting above ~6.2 clamped silently to the device maximum and the front
    end sat pinned wide open. Also warns when the device applies a materially
    different gain than was requested; silent clamping is how this went
    unnoticed, since readsb reported the gain it asked for, never the gain it
    got.

  * NOT claimed: that this makes a LimeSDR decode ADS-B. It fixes a units bug
    that is wrong on any SoapySDR device. A separate claim in the upstream
    commit message -- that the Lime delivers 12-bit data in the CS16 container
    roughly 18 dB below full scale -- has since been MEASURED FALSE: sampling
    the raw stream at gain 60 gives peak 31216 of 32767, i.e. the driver does
    scale to full 16-bit range. That earlier figure was taken with the front
    end clamped by this very bug.

 -- Greg Albrecht <oss@snstac.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000

